WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.360
Posters are often the first way to get moviegoers
excited for the next big release.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:08.180
The trick for a studio's marketing team is
to tease something cool about their upcoming

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.220
movies without giving away too much.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.000
Considering that a poster is just one picture,
you would think that wouldn't be too difficult.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.020
But sometimes designers get a little too excited
and present an image that somehow manages

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.760
to reveal way too much.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.040
Maybe they thought audiences wouldn't notice
or wouldn't care, but they can't deny that

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.480
these posters ruined the entire movie.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.220
In case the title of this video didn’t tip
you off, warning, massive movie spoilers ahead.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.480
Prometheus

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.190
There's a lot about Prometheus that audiences
might find confusing, and that's beyond Michael

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.600
Fassbender's unnerving portrayal of an android
named David.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.920
Character motivations are murky, the setting
is sometimes obscure, and the whole thing

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.980
is supposed to be a prequel to Alien.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.600
There are plenty of quiet moments, which are
not common in a modern sci-fi movie, but eventually

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:59.040
Prometheus gets around to what we all go to
the movies to see: big-budget, CGI explosions.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.120
The film's action peaks when two gleaming,
futuristic spaceships crash into each other

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.660
while humans watch from below.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:09.580
You might recognize this climactic, eye-popping
scene from the poster, as the explosion is

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.620
the key image.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:11.900
Split

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.160
M. Night Shyamalan's Unbreakable stars Bruce
Willis as a Philadelphia man named David Dunn

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.840
who comes to realize that he's nearly invincible,
while crossing paths with his villainous opposite,

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.130
the fragile-boned Elijah Price, a.k.a. Mr.
Glass.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:30.000
When it was released in 2000, Willis announced
that the film was part of a planned trilogy.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.160
But a sequel never materialized... until it
did, when nobody was looking.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:39.440
In 2017, Shyamalan had his biggest hit in
years with the psychological thriller Split,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:44.600
starring James McAvoy as a disturbed man with
24 separate personalities who kidnaps a trio

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:45.900
of teenage girls.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.800
At the end, he escapes, and viewers learn
more details about the man on the loose via

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.800
a news report playing on a TV in a diner.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.860
"And they gave him a funny name, too.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:57.820
What was it?"

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.260
"Mr. Glass.”

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.340
Split was set in the same universe as Unbreakable
all along.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:05.180
Who knew?

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.280
Fans who remembered both posters, that's who.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.320
The two images share a virtually identical
breaking glass pattern.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.879
The average moviegoer might have missed it,
but for Shyamalan diehards, the Split poster

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.120
gave away its big reveal ahead of time.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:20.020
Avengers: Infinity War

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.540
In the box office behemoth Avengers: Infinity
War, intergalactic villain Thanos completes

00:02:25.559 --> 00:02:27.799
his savage quest for the Infinity Stones.

00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:32.249
He uses them to instantaneously wipe out half
of all life in the universe, including many

00:02:32.249 --> 00:02:35.300
of the superheroes assembled by the Avengers
to stop him.

00:02:35.329 --> 00:02:40.239
For a studio to kill off so many beloved money-makers
always comes as something of a surprise, even

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.069
if most of them will presumably come back
to life in the sequel.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.860
But if you were a Japanese-fluent fan who
saw the Japanese poster, you had those sacrifices

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.180
spoiled by the tagline, which translates to
"Avengers, totally annihilated."

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.040
"I don't wanna go.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.880
I don’t wanna go, sir, please.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.440
Please, I don’t wanna go.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:58.420
I don’t wanna go."

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:03.160
Deadpool 2

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.419
Both the content and marketing of the Deadpool
films are rife with sarcasm, self-awareness,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:09.520
and self-deprecation.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.080
That silliness was on full display in the
Deadpool 2 poster, which looks like a '60s

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.500
Italian spy movie crossed with Smokey and
the Bandit.

00:03:16.769 --> 00:03:21.020
Such a piece of promotional art would have
to include the funniest, smarmiest, and most

00:03:21.029 --> 00:03:22.620
sarcastic tagline possible.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.029
While the Deadpool team could have just spoiled
their own movie, they instead decided to reveal

00:03:27.029 --> 00:03:30.750
the ending of another movie set in Fox's X-Men
universe.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:35.560
Logan, which was released a year before Deadpool
2, marked the last time Hugh Jackman would

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.060
lead a movie as the Wolverine.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.740
That fact was made quite clear by the Deadpool
2 poster, which featured the tagline "From

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.800
the studio that killed Wolverine."

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:45.060
Carrie

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:50.740
The 1976 teen horror classic Carrie is about
a teenage girl kept sheltered away from the

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:53.280
world by her domineering and deeply troubled
mother.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:53.940
Why?

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.160
Because Carrie can do powerful things with
her telekinetic mind.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.340
Insecure and socially awkward, she's bullied
by the other kids at school, and it all comes

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.480
to a head at the prom.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.920
Mean girl Norma gets Carrie elected prom queen
as a joke.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.639
Carrie thinks she's finally won over her horrible
classmates, until they soak her with a bucket

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.600
of pig's blood.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:16.419
That sets off Carrie, who uses the full extent
of her mental powers to kill pretty much everyone

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:16.920
present.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:23.220
It's the climax of the film, and it's revealed
on the poster: happy, prom queen Carrie juxtaposed

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.630
next to angry, blood-drenched Carrie.

